  "debriefMetadata": {
    "summary": "A 24-year-old with a known peanut allergy develops anaphylaxis after a restaurant meal. The case trains rapid clinical recognition, immediate IM epinephrine, safe route choice, supportive care, reassessment, and a biphasic-risk-aware disposition.",
    "keyTeachingPoints": [
      "Anaphylaxis is a clinical diagnosis — treat on recognition, no test is needed first (EV-ANA-001, EV-ANA-002)",
      "IM epinephrine into the anterolateral thigh comes first; delay worsens outcomes (EV-ANA-010, EV-ANA-011)",
      "Never give undiluted IV bolus epinephrine to a patient with a pulse (EV-ANA-015)",
      "Position supine, give high-flow oxygen when hypoxic, and bolus IV fluids for hypotension (EV-ANA-020, EV-ANA-021, EV-ANA-022)",
      "Antihistamines and steroids are adjuncts — they never substitute for epinephrine; reassess after each dose and observe for biphasic reactions (EV-ANA-012, EV-ANA-023, EV-ANA-024, EV-ANA-030)"
    ],
    "commonErrors": [
      "Treating the rash (antihistamine/steroid) instead of the reaction (epinephrine)",
      "Delaying epinephrine to finish the history or investigations",
      "Choosing the IV bolus route for epinephrine",
      "Discharging immediately after apparent recovery",
      "Never re-examining the patient after treatment"
    ]
  },
